AB  - Aerial conidia of 16 Beauveria bassiana strains originally derived from different host insects worldwide were separately suspended in a nutritional solution containing 2% sucrose and 0.5% peptone. For each of the strains, the conidial suspension of 1 mL in a 10-mL glass tube was exposed to a thermal stress of water bath at 48C for 45 min and then incubated by shaking at 25C for 24 h. The residue viability of each strain after the thermal stress was assessed as an index of its thermotolerance. As a result, mean residue viabilities in three assays, ranging from 4.6 to 87.1%, differed significantly among the 16 strains after exposure to the thermal stress. Of those, three strains were of high thermotolerance due to their residue viabilities of 40% but four were weak in thermotolerance due to their residue viabilities of 10%. The residue viabilities of the rest strains fell in a range of 11.0--23.6% and their thermotolerance was thus intermediate. When three strains with the residue viabilities of 40.8%, 15.8% and 4.6% after the thermal stress were grown separately on SDAY plates at 25, 30 and 35C, their colony diameters were well in accordance with the level of the thermotolerance. Only was the most thermotolerant strain capable of growing and sporulating at 35C, being unusual for B. bassiana, although colony diameters at 25C were not different among the three strains.
